mself  the landscape is littered with  burned-out cases  children of the 1950s who spent  themselves over the next two decades  like roman candles  but there is a survivor unrepentant and  unforgiving  lurking in a log cabin high in the  colorado rockies  hunter s thompson just reached the  landmark age of 50  and after a midlife low when it was  feared he was finished  the outlaw prince of gonzo journalism is  writing again  with the fury of a shark and a feeding  frenzy  so  those are all i have every time i do  something like this my wife tells me  you've gone on too long so uh i tried to  spare you some  long long  uh  reading but try to hit some highlights  tell some stories uh  we've got a couple of minutes if anybody  has a question i'll be happy to  try to try to deal with something in the  time remaining  curtis wilkie those are just fabulous  stories uh fabulous we'd like to let's  open it up for questions um  one quick observation that quote where  you have the simile like roman candles  do you remember the passage from jack  kerouac when he oh burn burn burn burn  burn  sure  you know uh on the road was yeah very  influential  to me i was a student at ole miss and  i actually quit school uh for a semester  and went on the road because of that  damn book and  the uh the only and unfortunately i  wrote two short stories that made their  way into publication they were both  awful  and i've burned any copy i've ever  encountered and knew that i didn't have  a future  in fiction  but um you know the kerouac and also the  fact that i did flunk uh  uh feature writing which is the course i  now teach today  uh encouraged me to drop out for a  semester and and it was very fortunate  because  i was back in ole miss i should have  already graduated i was back there for  all the excitement in the fall of 62  when james merritt  was finally admitted with the help of  about 30 000 troops  truly extraordinary uh experience  but sure i remember uh burn burn burn  yeah hey we're going to go to him for  this i want to get out one quick  question um  the uh north towards home then the  willie morrisses who went went  up east we had a number of reporters  come through this market um  halberstam um i mean the list goes on  and on um  who southerners who ended up being quite  successful on the east coast in in  mainstream media environments up there  what accounted for that  well i think so many of us felt driven  to get out of there in the 60s because  it had been so bad so intolerable and  god knows what it was like for uh for  for blacks  uh and i was i was there in mississippi  delta and and covered it for  nearly seven years and i had my snoop  full of it and i left and i'll never  forget driving to washington  uh as we passed into memphis leaving  mississippi i said free at last free at  last thank god almighty free at last  and now i'm back there and back there  and very very happy because it's so much  better uh  where they really beat me back willie's  book was also very influential to me i  read that when i was still living in  clarksdale and it i really related  to it and willie became a dear friend  and uh  willie encouraged me to write a book  called dixie that is uh  kind of a reverse north toward home it's  about leaving and  going back to the south because i had  seen the transformation that had gone on  in our region and  i found i was comfortable  with with the south again and i love  being back there you know david  halperstein another dear dear friend of  mine david  started his career in mississippi  and of course uh didn't last but a year  came here to the tennessean  and uh you know the rest is history but  david had an affinity with mississippi  until the day he died he  uh spent a great deal of time in  mississippi he was our commencement  speaker  uh at ole miss uh he come down and visit  me and go to football games his  daughter worked in the mississippi delta  for teach for america and uh david's  widow just 