 if you've got a beat for  example a sports beat you know  sports people are particularly sensitive  about uh adverse  coverage you know they're the worst you  know you can't say anything about a  coach or a player and you know they'll  try to cut you off  but uh  uh  you know i shouldn't you know i just say  you don't let that deter you just keep  on keep after it because  you know it's nothing like good honest  coverage of  things uh  whether it's sports or whether it's it's  politics  um  i have one final question and then we'll  we'll probably call it a day but in my  experience the most sensitive people  were restaurant owners i've been sued  as a publisher i've been sued more by  restaurant owners than anybody else  um what do you think of media today  at print media and could you do today  what you did then  it you know  we're in a you know kind of discouraging  situation economically so we're not as  strong as we once were  um  there  it is a struggling profession if we are  a profession some people say we're not  you know we're  a craft or maybe worse than that  um  there continues to be a lot of very good  journalism going on today unfortunately  there's not as  much of it  for example  the boston globe i can speak uh best  about it when uh  when i left there in and retired in 2000  we had i believe seven overseas bureaus  uh  the new york times had bought the globe  uh they they shut them down  um the networks uh  they they have very few  overseas networks today  i mean you'll see a  a story maybe from berlin and  and the guy who is covering it is  is in london you know he's kind of their  european  correspondent so it's not just  newspapers it's  it's everybody and and we're hurt by uh  by economics  they're still trying to come to grips  with uh  with the internet uh thank god i left  before all that yet i i i can barely do  an email i  i don't do facebook and tweets and  twitters and i i don't understand what's  going on in journalism today but  there 